<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="NAD(P)/NADPH interconversion" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Ubiquitous Plant Pathways (UPP) that was generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most land plants. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; The pyridine nucleotides coenzymes NAD and NADP are omnipresent in all living organisms and are involved in a vast amount of reactions with an enormous variety of corresponding enzymes (&gt;17% of all classified enzymes). The majority of these enzymes exhibit absolute stereospecificity, and use the coenzymes as acceptors for a hydride ion (H-) or an equivalent thereof originating from the reaction substrate. In most cases there is a specific interaction between the enzyme and the coenzyme, causing a change in the coenzyme stereochemistry. Based on this fact the pyridine nucleotide coenzymes are sometimes referred to as &quot;cosubstrates&quot; |CITS: [3157549]|. The dynamics and regulative aspects of both phosphorylation and desphosphorylation of NAD and NADH are not well understood. Whereas the importance of e.g. NAD(H) and NADP(H) is obvious through their close involvement in energy metabolism and reductive biosynthesis, their interconversion and the role of some of the participating enzymes requires further research. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; NAD kinases are found in all organisms indicating a crucial role for this enzyme. The enzymes have been discussed in the context of maintaining a physiological balance of reactive oxygen species (ROS) generated in mitochondria |CITS: [15247403]|, where significant changes in the NADP+/NAD+ ratio are observed upon oxidative stress |CITS: [11337409]|. On the other side a novel NADH kinase has been identified in |FRAME:TAX-3701| which is not associated with mitochondria and related metabolic processes. This NADH kinase has been discussed in connection with an increased metabolic flexibility of nicotinamide metabolism by uncoupling NADP+ synthesis from the redox status of the corresponding pool. However, further research is required to precisely describe its physiological role in the cell |CITS: [15347288]|. The interconversion of NAD+ to NAD(H) can be performed by two enzymes that are included in this pathway, the |FRAME: MONOMER-11877| (E.C. 1.6.1.1) where another pyridine nucleotide redox carrier is the acceptor of the hydride ion |CITS: [10548498]|, and the |FRAME: CPLX-5263| (E.C. 1.6.5.3), (also referred to as complex I), which couples oxidation of NAD(H) to the reduction of ubiquinone (and vice versa) |CITS: [12837548]|. The hydrolysis of NADP and NADPH in plants is carried out by corresponding phosphatases in dependence on the plant developmental stages and metabolic requirements. The |FRAME: MONOMER-11869| activity correlates with the dormancy state of |FRAME: TAX-4498| caryopses being involved in the maintenance of steady ratios of NADP and NADPH during seed imbibition, in spite of likewise increased NAD kinase activities |CITS: [10944152]|. While all of the processes and reactions mentioned so far are universal, the last two enzymes in this pathway have been described only in certain eukaryotic organisms. |FRAME: CPLX-7016| has been described only in green plants and several yeast species |CITS: [15347288][642063][ 642066]|, and |FRAME: MONOMER-11870| was described only in |FRAME: TAX-4577| |CITS: [16658848]|.</Description>
